> until I got to the 'partition hard drive' stage. At which point an error
> message comes up saying it cannot find the file storage area to
> partition, after you click 'ok' it shuts-down and goes back to the SRM
> console.
> 

> I am now convinced the problem lies with the RAID controller. It is
> *not* a Mylex DAC960 as it stamped on the actual board, it is actually a
> RAID array 230/Plus (KZPAC), which is apparantly Alpha's own controller
> that they made from Mylex-stamped boards... (For info on this controller

The KZPAC is actually _is_ a Mylex DAC 960, and it can be flashed with
the PC firmware provided by Mylex, even when it is used in an Alpha.
As the linux DAC 960 driver will not work with firmware revisions prior
to 2.73, you will have to use the PC firmware.
Use the firmware loader and the manual provided on [1] to flash the
firmware found on [2]

> So I have now upgraded the firmware for the controller, as well as
> having reconfigured the attached hard-drives. They are configured as 2
> JBODs. All of which goes very smoothly and follows the documentation to
> the letter. Having done this I then attempted to re-install RedHat using
> noprobe command. ie boot dkb0 -file /kernels/vmlinux.gz -flags
> "root=/dev/scd0 initrd=images/cdrom.img noprobe" . This installs every
> driver I have except the misnamed DAC960. The DAC960 is in the list but
> it refuses to recognise it (likely because it's not one) and it comes
> back with an error message.

As mentioned above, this seems to be rather problem of the firmware
revision than one of the hardware. Thus, with the most actual firmware,
the controller should work fine.

Alternatively, if you will not succeed to install RedHat, I could provide
you with my homebrewed debian installation disks for the AS 1200.

[1] http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/products/storage/ra230/index.html
[2] http://www.mylex.com/pub/support/archive/dac960_pci/fwpd273.exe

hth, regards
  Michael Keller
  (happily running debian woody on his AS 1200 with a Mylex DAC 960)
