Tom Brown <x.x.x.x@optusnet.com.au> wrote:
> Thanks a heap Michael Keller, I did as you instructed and RedHat recognised
> the raid controller! Installation seems to be going quite smoothly now, no
> further problems to report.
> 
> About the DEC 3000, it has an OS on it, Tru64 4.0. The only problem is
> finding a monitor that works with it. Although I have recently been told I
> can hook up a serial cable from the back of my PC to the back of the server
> and telnet into it.

Not really "telnet to it". With things configured properly you should
get a text console hooked to your 3000 by a serial line.  In that sense
it is "local".  Any half-decent serial terminal or a terminal emulator
on another machine should do.

> The only immediate problem to this seems to be that the
> PC has a normal adaptor whilst the DEC 3000 has some thing that looks like a
> telephone line connection.

This could be for some fixed frequency monitor.  I really do not know
what kind of a graphic display support you may have on your Turbochannel
(hence not PCI) machine.

You have another option especially if you have another machine running
X on your local network (like this Linux system you are installing right
now).  Run 3000 headless and display X on a monitor hooked up to this
other system.  There are various ways to do it (cf. 'man xon', for
example) but ssh is likely the simplest one.  If from a running X
session you ssh to your 3000 then whatever X client you will start there
will display automagically on your original host (assuming that you
are not blocking X forwarding and it is by a default on; otherwise you
would need adjust configurations and/or use extra options to ssh ;
see 'man ssh' and 'man sshd' for more details).

X is a protocol designed from the very beginning to work over networks
and you can do the above across half of the world although then it
likely will be slow. :-)

  Michal


