> also another question, why doesnt it boot rightly to linux? (i've 
> choosen for aboot so no multi-system boot i've only installed
> debian). now i'm booting it from a boot floppy >>> boot dva0 -flags 0
> 
> 

During installation, I have selected to install aboot on the boot disk
(DKA200), and all works fine. If You don't make it, see aboot man page
for how make it.

This is my aboot.conf:

io:/# cat /etc/aboot.conf
0:1/vmlinuz ro root=/dev/sdc1 floppy=0,4,cmos
2:1/vmlinuz-2.2.20 ro root=/dev/sdc1 floppy=0,4,cmos
3:1/vmlinuz-2.2.22 ro root=/dev/sdc1 floppy=0,4,cmos
4:1/vmlinuz-2.4.18-generic ro root=/dev/sdc1 initrd=/initrd.img
floppy=0,4,cmos (wrapped line !)

the default boot flag of my machine is:
boot dka200 -fl 0 (starts 2.4.18 self compiled)

the other kernels are started with:
boot dka200 -fl 2 (starts 2.2.20)
boot dka200 -fl 3 (starts 2.2.22)
boot dka200 -fl 4 (starts 2.4.18 generic)

the floppy=0,4,cmos kernel command is needed because my 1.44 floppy is
detected as 2.88 MB, see /var/log/messages to check this.

Regards

GuidoS1966



