Guillaume Dargaud <news_colostate@gdargaud.net> wrote:
> Would an online upgrade using rsync be better (or safer) than a CD upgrade ?

'rsync' is one of possible protocols to get data of the net.  It has
various advantages (for example, you can use '--partial' and will
restart automagically broken transfers).  Anaconda, i.e. Red Hat
installer, does not have an option to use rsync for an installation.
In general option to install over a network are really valuable but
most often not practical if not used on a local subnetwork.

> I have a hard time finding a recent Milo.

Try http://www.suse.de/~stepan/.  There is a chance that these
versions are good enough.  Still, for example, booting an installation
CD from SRM looks like that:

  b <your_cd_device_here> -fl 0

With milo it may require a bit of creativity on your part (very old
instructions taken literally do not work).

> I found some documentation that
> indicates which MILO to upgrade for my system, but I haven't been able to
> find the file to download (lx164.img).

If your machine is LX then SRM firmware for it should be available.

> Now what happens if I make a floppy with the latest SRM ?

You can switch firmware loading it to NVRAM from floppy.  There
should be READMEs together with firmware describing how to do it.
Also visit http://www.alphalinux.org/

> Will it replace
> MILO and that's it or will I need to 'play' with it for a while to be able
> to boot my current config,

If you will replace MILO with SRM then you will be not able to boot from
your current hard disk as ARC/Milo expects to see FAT-type partitioning
on that disk while SRM wants to have a disklabel.  Firmware limitations.
You can still boot what you have right now from a floppy, CD or another
hard disk.

Often it is possible to replace a partition table without touching
your file systems and data, or only with a minimal disturbunce, but
this is an operation of a kind "if you need to ask how to do that then
you do not want to play with it".  A safe course is
backup-reformat-restore.

> It looks like a dangerous procedure (that's the "AlphaBIOS Update" in the
> boot menu, right ?)

Updating firmware is not (yes, this is the right menu).  Playing with
file systems might be.

I did not touch LX for a long time but I remember that it was possible
to load SRM from a floppy on a "temporary basis", for experiments,
and after a reboot one was back in ARC.  I do not remember details
how it was done though but it was pretty obvious.

>> How much space you have available?
> Currently none, but I should be able to get 2Gb by changing the partitions.

That should be more than enough unless you are trying to install
"everything".

  Michal


