>>
>> This warning tells you that you have to add this missing piece of info
>> to your configuration (check with 'lspci' what values).  Yes, I guess
>> that a wording of that could be somewhat improved. :-)
> 
> here is the output from "lspci -v"
> 00:0c.0 Display controller: Digital Equipment Corporation DECchip 21030
> [TGA] (rev 02)
>        Flags: bus master, stepping, medium devsel, latency 255, IRQ 14
>        Memory at 000000000a000000 (32-bit, prefetchable)
>        Expansion ROM at 0000000082000000 [disabled]

00:0c.0 in hex is 0:12.0 in decimal so X server seems to be somewhat
smarter than it pretends to be. :-)  I wonder why it is not using this
knowledge.  Maybe there is no way to make it reliable?  I would add a line

  BusID "PCI:0:12:0"

between 'Section "Device"' and 'EndSection' of your XF86Config-4
file and would try again (not really sure if double qoutes are really
required).  In case of failure looking carefuly through
/var/log/XFree86.0.log, and similar, files may give better ideas
(wrong sync values?, clocks? modes out of range of your equipment?).

Anyway, looking at 'man XF86Config' one can find, among many other
things:

       BusID  "bus-id"
              This specifies the bus location of the graphics card.  For
              PCI/AGP   cards,   the   bus-id   string   has   the  form
              PCI:bus:device:function (e.g., "PCI:1:0:0" might be appro�
              priate  for  an AGP card).  This field is usually optional
              in  single-head  configurations  when  using  the  primary
              graphics  card.   In  multi-head  configurations,  or when
              using a secondary graphics card in a single-head  configu�
              ration,  this  entry is mandatory.  Its main purpose is to
              make an unambiguous connection between the device  section
              and the hardware it is representing.  This information can
              usually be found by running the X server with the -scanpci
              command line option.

As mentioned previously for TGA cards, AFAIK, this field is not optional
in any configuration.  See also /usr/X11R6/lib/X11/doc/README.DECtga
for other possible problems and solutions (MemBase expected?).

   Michal


