To potential best-friend,

I am writing this as I am almost at my wit's end trying to get RedHat
7.2(for alpha) onto my AlphaServer 1200. I live in Brisbane Australia. My
story started when I purchased an old Alpha DEC 3000 off ebay. Because the
monitor was so old and heavy I did not have it shipped with the comp even
though it was available. When all was said and done it cost me $230 US.
Converted to Australian dollars that was around $420. I then spent ages
phoning and driving the server around trying to find a compatible monitor.
Nothing! I even phoned up HP, they quoted me more than $2000 for a new one
:) So the DEC 3000 is still in the closet without a monitor...

Anyway, I made a mental note to check everything twice before buying another
server. But as luck would have it I found a place in my city that sells
second hand servers. I only went there looking for a monitor for the damn
DEC 3000, I didn't even know they sold servers. Anyway, the monitor they
had, whilst having 3 RGB plugs and that was 'certainly' going to work,
didn't. The guy mentioned they had an Alpha 1200 for sale that they didn't
know much about. He quoted me $450 (like $250 US). We had to bust the lock
open to look inside and I couldn't believe my luck. 533Mhz with dual
capability. 512MB RAM. 2 9GB hard drives with slots for 5 more, etc. I
wanted to put Linux on my this server as the DEC has/had Tru64 (well so I am
told, having never seen the damn thing operate myself). So I checked before
I bought it that it would accept RedHat, everything I read about it said
yes.

So...I purchased Red Hat 7.2 for alpha, which is on 4 disks. The
installation of which went very smoothly at first. I got the installation
info from http://www.support.compaq.com/alpha-tools/redhat/ig/  It went
smoothly until I got to the 'partition hard drive' stage. At which point an
error message comes up saying it cannot find the file storage area to
partition, after you click 'ok' it shuts-down and goes back to the SRM
console.

I am now convinced the problem lies with the RAID controller. It is *not* a
Mylex DAC960 as it stamped on the actual board, it is actually a RAID array
230/Plus (KZPAC), which is apparantly Alpha's own controller that they made
from Mylex-stamped boards... (For info on this controller goto
http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/products/storage/ra230/index.html ).
This alone caused weeks of useless running around and almost caused me to
load the wrong drivers for it which obviously would have been disasterous.

So I have now upgraded the firmware for the controller, as well as having
reconfigured the attached hard-drives. They are configured as 2 JBODs. All
of which goes very smoothly and follows the documentation to the letter.
Having done this I then attempted to re-install RedHat using noprobe
command. ie boot dkb0 -file /kernels/vmlinux.gz -flags "root=/dev/scd0
initrd=images/cdrom.img noprobe" . This installs every driver I have except
the misnamed DAC960. The DAC960 is in the list but it refuses to recognise
it (likely because it's not one) and it comes back with an error message.

My obvious question, before I totally give up on Alpha's, is where can I go
from here? Are there any drivers for RedHat available for the controller I
have? Will I have to bite the bullet and buy a new controller? If so which
kind? Am I missing something simple/stupid? Any help on this would be
greatly appreciated and I thank you a lot just for taking the time to read
this.

Sincerely,
Tim.





